From 3fb59a99b053af4df34eacdc10698c05a26d257a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HuskerMinion <28571744+HuskerMinion@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/40] PCI: rtd129x: Add the Realtek RTD129x PCIe host
 controller
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add a host driver for the PCIe 1.1 ("slot 1") and PCIe 2.0 ("slot 2")
root complexes of the Realtek RTD1293/RTD1295/RTD1296 SoCs.

The link partner sits directly on the root bus, its configuration space
is accessed through the controller's indirect configuration engine, and
legacy interrupts are routed to one GIC SPI per slot via interrupt-map.

On revision A0x chips CPU accesses to the 16 MiB PCIe memory windows
(0xc0000000-0xc1ffffff) read as all ones, so for example AHCI fails
with "Controller reset failed (0xffffffff)". Like the QNAP and Synology
vendor kernels, route every device MMIO access through the controller's
translated 4 KiB window instead: select the PCI page with PCI_TRANS
(and a 2 KiB window for the offsets 0xcec/0xc68 that the vendor code
avoids), access the window, and retry reads on a latched completion
error. With CONFIG_PCIE_RTD129X_MMIO_HOOK, ioremap() of the windows
returns the bus address as a cookie and the arm64 raw MMIO accessors
hand such cookies to the driver. This costs one address compare per
MMIO access, and BARs cannot be mapped to user space. The hook is
compiled out for code that is not part of the kernel proper or must not
call into it (the KVM nVHE/VHE hypervisor objects, the EFI stub and the
early position-independent code); none of it accesses PCIe memory. The
driver depends on ARM64, where the hook lives.

The window registers are read back after each update, so the new
translation is in place before the access. PCI_TRANS is programmed with
CPU addresses, so probe rejects a memory window with a CPU-to-bus
offset.

Config accesses poll the completion status with interrupts off under
the controller lock. A completion normally arrives within microseconds,
so poll every 5 us for at most 1 ms per attempt; with the retries on a
latched completion error the worst case stays at a few milliseconds.

The bring-up sequence, PHY tuning tables, SB4 revision quirk and
completion-error ("DLLP error patch") retry follow the Realtek vendor
drivers (QNAP GPL 4.2.8 and Realtek BSP 4.9, by James Tai) and Andreas
Färber's WIP driver, reworked to use the host bridge, reset, GPIO and
syscon APIs; the copyright lines of the Realtek BSP driver and of
Andreas Färber's driver are kept. Unlike the vendor code, the clock is
ungated before the resets are released, because the boot loader leaves
the PCIe clock gates off and slot 1 then does not train. PERST# (if
described) is held from the start and released 200 ms (slot 1) or
100 ms (slot 2) after the PHY setup, the delays the vendor code uses
even without a PERST# GPIO, and the link gets 300 ms to come up.

There is no RTD129x clock driver yet, so the clock is optional; without
one, the clock-enable bit is set through the CRT syscon. The
controller registers are accessed through the RBUS erratum helpers.

To help with bring-up on other boards, the driver logs the SB2
0x30-0x60 PCI registers and decodes MAC_ST when the link does not come
up.

After the link is up, the driver waits PCIE_RESET_CONFIG_WAIT_MS before
the first configuration request, as the PCIe specification requires;
the ASMedia SATA controller on slot 2 does not answer earlier.
